diagnosis_text:
The user's complaint — that pc0 (20.1.1.x) cannot reach pc12 (120.1.1.x) via the intermediate "border post" — is directly contradicted by all available evidence. Phase 1 reachability testing found 0% packet loss in both directions with sub-millisecond RTT. A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep confirms the entire forwarding path is healthy.

The "border post" the user describes is r11, an OSPF-EIGRP dual-protocol boundary router. r11 runs OSPF on its eth1 (111.1.1.2/8, area 3) to connect to the core OSPF domain (r1 side, where pc0 lives), and runs EIGRP AS 100 on eth0 (112.1.1.1/8) and eth2 (103.1.1.1/8) to connect to r12 and r13. Its FRR configuration carries `redistribute eigrp` inside the OSPF stanza, which injects EIGRP-learned prefixes — including 120.0.0.0/8 (pc12's network) — into the OSPF topology. r1's routing table confirms it holds an active OSPF route to 120.0.0.0/8 via r11 (111.1.1.2), last refreshed 11+ minutes ago. r12's EIGRP adjacency with r11 is Full, and pc12 reaches pc0 via r12's default route through r11. No ACL drop rules, NAT rules, traffic-control shaping, link faults, or process crashes were found on any device.

Root cause: No fault — the reported symptoms are not reproducible. The OSPF-EIGRP redistribution on r11 is active and correct; both directions of the pc0↔pc12 path carry traffic without loss.

Fix: No corrective action is required. If the user experienced a brief outage at the time of the ticket, it is consistent with normal routing convergence immediately after lab startup (all FRR processes initialised at 07:32).
